===Proper noun===
{{en-proper noun|s|Caesares}}

# An ancient Roman family name, notably that of [[w:Julius Caesar|Julius Caesar]].
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:William Rainey Harper; Herbert Cushing Tolman|title=Eight Books of Caesar’s Gallic War|location=New York, N.Y.; Cincinnati, Oh.; Chicago, Ill.|publisher=[[w:American Book Company (1890)|American Book Company]]|year=1891|page=2|passage=The '''Caesars''' were an ancient and aristocratic family, which for eight generations had been prominent in the commonwealth.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|journal=Papers and Monographs of the [[w:American Academy in Rome]]|volume=16|year=1955|page=100|passage=Habel, 63 ff., believes that up to 51 the emperors bestowed priesthoods on the '''Caesares''', using their special power of commendation; but that these '''Caesares''' were forced to wait until a place in a college was vacant; that after 51 the '''Caesares''' were elected by the senate.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Taylor Caldwell|title=A Pillar of Iron|publisher=[[w:Doubleday (publisher)|Doubleday]]|year=1965|page=128|isbn=9780449239520|passage=To the powerful, the '''Caesares''' were flattering, deferential, sincerely devoted, self-sacrificing, loyal, eagerly serving.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|journal=Collection Latomus|volume=142|year=1975|page=39|passage=Five of the '''Caesares''' were killed during the years 90-85 (''D. G.'', 3, 114).}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|journal=w:Antichthon|volume=34–36|year=1996|page=56|passage={{...}} ''Off.'' 1.133 and ''Att.'' 13.19.4 do not establish the uterine relationship of Catulus and the '''Caesares''', merely the fact that Catulus and the '''Caesares''' were regarded as ''fratres''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Friedrich Münzer|tlr=Thérèse Ridley|title=Roman Aristocratic Parties and Families|publisher=w:Johns Hopkins University Press|year=1999|page=319|isbn=9780801859908|passage= Of the two plebeian candidates Silanus obviously counted on the assistance of Manlius, who was in office and to whom he was related from the grandfather, but his rival, a Minucius who had become a Marcius, counted on the patrician rival Caesar and on the other consul, L. Aurelius Cotta, because the '''Caesares''' were related by marriage both with the Marcii and the Cottae; in fact, Silanus was defeated by their coalition.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Richard W[alter] Hooper|title=Representative Chapters in Ancient History: From Romulus to Justinian|publisher=w:University Press of America|year=2000|page=542|isbn=9780761818199|passage=The Pulchri were a branch of the Clodius family, just as the '''Caesares''' were of the Julii.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Ernst Badian|chapter=From the Iulii to Caesar|editor=[[w:Miriam T. Griffin|Miriam Griffin]]|title=A Companion to Julius Caesar|publisher=[[w:Wiley-Blackwell|Wiley Blackwell]]|year=2009|page=15|isbn=978-1-4051-4923-5|passage=He was joined on this commission by C. Caesar Strabo (''RE'' Iulius no. 135), of another branch of the '''Caesares''', who had stressed his heritage by adding the old Julian ''praenomen'' Vopiscus (see the ''cos.'' 473, ''MRR'' 1.29) to his name as a ''cognomen''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|editors=w:Simon Hornblower; Antony Spawforth; w:Esther Eidinow|title=[[w:Oxford Classical Dictionary|The Oxford Classical Dictionary]]|edition=4th|publisher=w:Oxford University Press|year=2012|page=755|isbn=978-0-19-954556-8|passage=By about the middle of the 2nd cent., the '''Caesares''' were claiming descent from *Ascanius and, through him, from his grandmother Venus (see the coins Crawford, ''RRC'' 258, 129 {{smallcaps|bc}}, and 320, 103 {{smallcaps|bc}}, the latter with a symbolic reference to Apollo, the ancient god of the ''gens'').}}
# {{lb|en|figuratively}} The government; society; earthly powers.
#* {{RQ:King James Version|Matthew|22|21|passage=Render therefore vnto '''Ceſar''', the things which are '''Ceſar'''s: and vnto God, the things that are Gods.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=The past and present life of the globe|page=9|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=LjwDAAAAQAAJ|author=David Page|year=1861|passage=let it be clearly understood that we are dealing with Life solely in its geological aspects. We appeal unto '''Caesar'''; let us be judged by '''Caesar'''’s laws.|pageurl=http://books.google.com/books?id=LjwDAAAAQAAJ&pg=PA9&dq=%22Caesar%27s+laws%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=Py3jUfTbNofa4AOQ_ICoAg&ved=0CJABEOgBMBE4eA#v=onepage&q=%22Caesar%27s%20laws%22&f=false}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|journal=Awake|volume=38|issue=14|page=6|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=bsAEAAAAYAAJ|year=1957|passage='''Caesar''' may discriminate unjustly against certain races. Christians are not to take issue with '''Caesar''''s laws on such matters and flout them, but should submit.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=On Doing Local History|page=76|url=https://books.google.com/books?isbn=0759102538|author=Carol Kammen|year=2003|passage=It is the story of churches that split apart over this issue and of ministers finding ways to justify the return of slaves because they were under the aegis of the laws of '''Caesar''', not the laws of God.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=God Is My Broker|url=https://books.google.com/books?isbn=0307799557|author=Christopher Buckley|year=2012|passage=But I know that '''Caesar''''s laws have been broken, and someone has to pay. I'm your man. These are good monks. If they committed any crime, it was to believe in me.|pageurl=http://books.google.com/books?id=OrEoO6eiIvcC&pg=PT204&dq=%22Caesar%27s+laws%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=LizjUZ6zKrPI4APgloBI&ved=0CDoQ6AEwATha}}

